  <h2>A quick word on the numbers</h2>
  <p>Before any figures: treat every number here as a rough shape, not a promise. Architecture pay varies hugely by country, by city, by the size and type of practice, by the wider economy and by the year you happen to be reading this. A salary in central London looks very different from one in a small regional studio or another country entirely. The ranges below are deliberately broad and are meant to show relative progression - how each stage typically compares to the last - rather than to quote a precise going rate. Always sense-check against current local sources before you make decisions.</p>

  <h2>The career path</h2>
  <p>Architecture has a fairly recognisable ladder, even though titles differ between practices. You usually start as a Part 1 assistant after your first degree, become a Part 2 assistant after your master's, then qualify as an architect once you pass Part 3. From there you can grow into a senior or project architect, then into associate roles that carry more responsibility for running projects and people, and eventually into director or partner level where you help lead the business itself. Each step typically brings more pay, but also more responsibility for deadlines, clients, staff and, higher up, the health of the practice as a whole.</p>

  <table class="data"><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Typically involves</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Part 1 assistant</td><td>First job after undergraduate degree; supporting project work</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Part 2 assistant</td><td>Post-master's; more design responsibility, working toward Part 3</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Qualified architect</td><td>Registered; running elements of projects independently</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Senior / project architect</td><td>Leading projects and mentoring junior staff</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Associate</td><td>Shared responsibility for projects, clients and team</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Director / partner</td><td>Leading the practice and its business decisions</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2>How pay tends to move</h2>
  <p>The pattern most people see is modest early pay that steps up meaningfully at two moments: qualifying as an architect after Part 3, and moving into senior or associate roles. Part 1 and Part 2 assistant salaries are typically the lowest rungs, reflecting that you are still training. Qualifying usually brings a noticeable rise. The biggest jumps tend to come later, at associate and director level, where your earnings start to reflect business responsibility and, sometimes, a share in the practice's success. It is a slow build in the early years followed by steeper growth for those who take on leadership - which is worth knowing when you weigh the long training against the reward.</p>

  <div class="callout"><strong>Tip.</strong> When comparing offers, look past the headline number: location and cost of living, overtime culture, support for your Part 3, professional membership fees and genuine progression can matter more to your real quality of life than a slightly higher salary.</div>

  <h2>Why the spread is so wide</h2>
  <p>Two architects at the same career stage can earn very different amounts, and it is not random. Location dominates - big expensive cities pay more in cash but cost more to live in. Practice type matters too: a large commercial firm, a boutique design studio and the public sector all pay differently and offer different lifestyles. Your own specialism and skills, from BIM management to sector expertise, can lift your value. And the economic cycle is real - construction is sensitive to downturns, which can freeze pay and hiring, then rebound. This is exactly why fixing on a single "average salary" is misleading.</p>

  <h2>Beyond the salary</h2>
  <p>Money is only part of the equation, and for many architects not the main part. People are often drawn to the profession for the creative work, the variety and the lasting result - a real building people use - rather than the pay, which is modest relative to the length of training. That is worth being honest with yourself about. It also helps to think in terms of a long arc: the early years are lean, but skills compound, and options widen as you gain experience, qualify and specialise. If you want the full picture of the training that underpins this ladder, see <a href="/career/how-to-become-an-architect/">how to become an architect</a>.</p>

  <h2>Doing your own research</h2>
  <p>Because figures shift and vary so much, the smartest move is to gather current, local data yourself. Professional bodies sometimes publish salary guidance, recruitment agencies release annual surveys, and talking honestly to people a few years ahead of you is often the most useful source of all. Triangulate a few sources rather than trusting one number, and revisit it periodically as you move through your career.</p>

  <div class="callout warn"><strong>Routes vary.</strong> Qualification paths, titles and figures differ by country and change over time - always check the current requirements of your national registration body (e.g. ARB/RIBA in the UK, NCARB in the US).</div>

